[00:08.78]This is the story of Joe and Fonzie.
[00:12.94]It’s a story about a friendship that started more than 20 years ago.
[00:18.76]It’s also a story about a friendship that saved a life and started a new kind of help for people with problems.
[00:28.52]‘Joe’ is Joe Hoagland, who was three years old when the story began.
[00:35.00]And ‘Fonzie’ is a 3-metre-long, 275 kilogram dolphin.
[00:43.01]When Joe was born, he had a problem with his heart and doctors had to operate on him.
[00:49.98]When he was three, he had another operation.
[00:54.36]This operation didn’t go well and he became paralysed – he couldn’t move the left side of his body.
[01:02.96]The doctors at the hospital didn’t think he could get better.
[01:08.02]Deena, Joe’s mother, took him to different places for therapy, but it wasn’t very successful.
[01:16.08]Then the family moved to Florida.
[01:19.34]Joe liked being in the water and Deena thought swimming could help him.
[01:25.44]First, they went to swimming pools in hotels, but some hotel guests didn’t like them being there.
[01:33.90]Then one day she went with Joe to a place in Key Largo called Dolphin Plus, where people swam with dolphins.
[01:44.16]Joe met Fonzie there and the story really began.
[01:49.52]Soon, Joe wanted to see Fonzie every day.
[01:54.60]He loved playing with him and feeding him.
[01:57.94]Every time that Joe arrived, Fonzie came to see him.
[02:03.00]Joe’s mother told him always to feed Fonzie using his left hand, so Joe used his left side more and more.
[02:13.54]Slowly, he got better and better.
[02:17.00]He’s now over 30 years old and very well.
[02:22.48]Deena saw that Joe’s friendship with Fonzie helped him a lot, and she knew it was a good idea for other people, too.
[02:32.02]So, she and her family worked to open a place called Island Dolphin Care.
[02:38.98]It’s next to Dolphin Plus and many people with health problems come to spend time with the dolphins.
[02:46.86]Sadly, Fonzie died in 2004 when Joe was 17.
[02:52.70]He wrote: ‘Fonzie was a great friend to me; he and I shared a lot of good times.
[02:59.78]He always put a smile on my face.’
